.²2 (<ab>Gram.</ab>) Extended application, application by analogy, transference
 of one attribute to another, attraction of one case or rule to another;
 {#atideSo nAma itaraDarmasya itarasmin prayogAya AdeSaH#} ({#mImAMsA#});
 or {#anyatrEva praRItAyAH kftsnAyA DarmasaMhateH . anyatra kAryataH prAptiratideSaH#}
 {#sa ucyate .. prAkftAt karmaRo yasmAttatsamAnezu karmasu . DarmapraveSo#}
 {#yena syAdatideSaH sa ucyate ..#} This {#atideSa#} is of 5 kinds: {#SAstra˚#},
 {#kArya˚, nimitta˚, vyapadeSa˚ & rUpa˚#}. Thus in Grammar {#prakftivat#}
 {#vikftiH, karmaRA karmavattulyakriyaH#} or {#puMvat, Ridvat, vyapadeSivadBAvaH#}
 & {#iRvadikaH#} are instances. {#gosadfSo gavayaH#} is an instance of
 {#rUpAtideSa#} or analogy; {#vAkyArTasyAtideSasya smftirvyApAra ucyate#}
 <ls>Bhāṣā P. 80</ls>. {#atideSa#} is generally expressed by words showing likeness
 or resemblance, such as {#iva, vat, sadfSa#} <ab>&c.</ab> <ab>cf.</ab> also {#atideSo nAma#}
 {#ye paratra vihitA DarmAstamatItyAnyatra tezAM deSaH .#} <ls>ŚB. on MS. 7. 1. 12</ls>.
 {#atideSa#} forms the subject mattar of the 7th and the 8th {#aDyAya#}s
 of {#jEmini#}ʼs {#mImAMsAsUtra#}. For its various divisions and subdivisions
 read: {#sa ca nAmnA vacanena vA . tatra nAma triviDamAtideSikaM karmanAma#},
 {#saMskAranAma, yOgikamiti . vacanaM punardviviDaM pratyakzaSrutamAnumAnikaM#}
 {#ca .#} (<ls>ŚB.</ls> <ab>ibid.</ab>).
